## Update Operations

What to test:

- Run the feed with `applyTransaction-update` and confirm the bar series mutates existing datums in place rather than appending.
- Compare against `updateDelta-update` and confirm both produce equivalent results.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Update" name="high-freq-update" type="generated" /%}

## Gap Filling

What to test:

- Run the feed and confirm the initial 30-59 minute gap fills in incrementally as datums arrive.
- Confirm no rendering glitches around the boundary between existing and newly-filled data.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Gap Filling" name="high-freq-gap-filling" type="generated" initialLoadDeferred=true /%}

## Stacked Line Series

The following example demonstrates the performance improvement of incremental grouping with stacked line charts. When using `applyTransaction()` with the new incremental grouping feature, the chart only processes new data points instead of regrouping all data. Compare the CPU usage between `updateDelta()` (full reprocessing) and `applyTransaction()` (incremental updates).

What to test:

- Run the feed under both `updateDelta()` and `applyTransaction()` and compare CPU usage in DevTools.
- Confirm the stacked line geometry remains identical between the two methods.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Stacked Line" name="high-freq-stacked-line" type="generated" /%}

## Multiple Charts

The following example demonstrates high-frequency updates across multiple area charts, simulating a monitoring dashboard for multiple systems. Each chart displays system and user CPU load with data updating every 50ms when the feed is running.

What to test:

- Start the feed and confirm every chart updates in lock-step at the 50ms cadence.
- Switch update method and confirm dashboard remains responsive across all charts.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Multi-Chart" name="high-freq-multi-chart" type="generated" /%}

## High Volume Line Series

This example is tuned for high-volume datasets. It starts with 100,000 data points and can add or remove batches of 100 points at a time. Use the controls to compare the cost of full `updateDelta()` refreshes against incremental `applyTransaction()` updates while the data stream maintains a constant window size.

What to test:

- Run the rolling-window feed under both update methods and compare per-tick cost.
- Change series type using the selector and confirm the comparison still holds.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency High Volume" name="high-freq-high-volume" type="generated" initialLoadDeferred=true /%}

## Random Updates

This example investigates the performance of `applyTransaction()` with randomized updates on large datasets. Unlike append/remove operations, this tests in-place value mutations where random data points are updated each tick. Compare the cost of incremental `applyTransaction({ update })` against full `updateDelta()` refreshes.

What to test:

- Run the feed and confirm randomly-mutated datums redraw in place without re-laying out the axis.
- Compare `applyTransaction({ update })` against `updateDelta()` and confirm both produce equivalent renders.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Random Update" name="high-freq-random-update" type="generated" initialLoadDeferred=true /%}

## Financial Charts

The following examples demonstrate high-frequency updates using the Financial Charts API (`AgCharts.createFinancialChart`). This uses the simplified financial chart options with built-in features like volume, navigator, range buttons, status bar, and zoom.

### Real-Time Data Feed

This example simulates a real-time data feed updating a financial chart with new datums.

What to test:

- Run the feed and confirm new candles and volume bars stream in together.
- Confirm built-in navigator, range buttons, and status bar continue to function while the feed runs.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Frequency Financial Chart" name="high-freq-financial-chart" type="generated" /%}

### High Volume Financial Chart

This example tests high-volume datasets with 100,000 data points using the Financial Charts API. Compare the performance of `applyTransaction()` vs `updateDelta()` for rolling window updates.

What to test:

- Run the rolling-window feed under both update methods and compare per-tick cost.
- Confirm zoom, navigator, and range buttons remain responsive at 100,000 points.

{% chartExampleRunner title="High Volume Financial Chart" name="high-freq-financial-high-volume" type="generated" initialLoadDeferred=true /%}

### Random Updates

This example investigates the performance of randomized updates on large financial chart datasets. Random datums are mutated each tick to test in-place value updates.

What to test:

- Run the feed and confirm randomly-mutated candles redraw in place without axis re-layout.
- Compare `applyTransaction` against `updateDelta` and confirm both produce equivalent renders.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Financial Chart Random Updates" name="high-freq-financial-random-update" type="generated" initialLoadDeferred=true /%}
